/-
# The Birkhoff model has Markov degree three (AIM computational algebraic statistics, Problem 1)

AIM workshop Computational algebraic statistics, problem list, Problem 1: explicitly describe a
Markov basis for tables with fixed first-order summary; in purely mathematical language, describe a
generating set for the toric ideal given by the `p² × p!` matrix whose columns are the `p!` `p × p`
permutation matrices.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Eval
public import Mathlib.Data.Finsupp.Weight
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Maps

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.Algebra.BirkhoffMarkovDegreeAIMComputationProblem16Wanted

/-!
Source: AIM workshop Computational algebraic statistics, problem list,
https://aimath.org/WWN/compalgstat/compalgstat.pdf, Problem 1. UnsolvedMath record UM-20001178
(`AIM-COMPUTATION-0016`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The toric ideal is the kernel of `K[p(σ) : σ ∈ S_p] → K[ψ_{jk}]`, `p(σ) ↦ ∏_j ψ_{j σ(j)}`, over an
arbitrary field `K` (as in Yamaguchi, Ogawa and Takemura). A binomial of degree `≤ d` is `x^u - x^v`
with `|u|, |v| ≤ d` and the same image. The formalized answer is the Markov degree: for `p ≥ 3` the
binomials of degree at most `3` generate the ideal and those of degree at most `2` do not (for `p ≤
2` the ideal is zero). The explicit list of moves is not formalized.
-/

open MvPolynomial

/-- The toric map of the Birkhoff model on `p` candidates over the field `K`: the indeterminate
`p(σ)` of a permutation `σ` of `Fin p` goes to the monomial `∏_j ψ_{j σ(j)}`. Its kernel is the
toric ideal of the `p² × p!` matrix whose columns are the `p × p` permutation matrices. -/
noncomputable def birkhoffToricMap (K : Type) [Field K] (p : ℕ) :
    MvPolynomial (Equiv.Perm (Fin p)) K →ₐ[K] MvPolynomial (Fin p × Fin p) K :=
  aeval fun σ => ∏ j : Fin p, X (j, σ j)

/-- The binomials `x^u - x^v` of degree at most `d` in the toric ideal of the Birkhoff model (`u, v`
exponent vectors of total degree `≤ d` whose monomials have the same image). -/
def birkhoffBinomials (K : Type) [Field K] (p d : ℕ) :
    Set (MvPolynomial (Equiv.Perm (Fin p)) K) :=
  {f | ∃ u v : Equiv.Perm (Fin p) →₀ ℕ, u.degree ≤ d ∧ v.degree ≤ d ∧
    birkhoffToricMap K p (monomial u 1) = birkhoffToricMap K p (monomial v 1) ∧
    f = monomial u 1 - monomial v 1}

/-- The Markov degree of the Birkhoff model is three: for `p ≥ 3` and every field `K`, the toric
ideal of the permutation matrices is generated by its binomials of degree at most `3` and not by
its binomials of degree at most `2`. -/
def birkhoffMarkovDegreeThree : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Type) [Field K] (p : ℕ), 3 ≤ p →
    RingHom.ker (birkhoffToricMap K p) = Ideal.span (birkhoffBinomials K p 3) ∧
    RingHom.ker (birkhoffToricMap K p) ≠ Ideal.span (birkhoffBinomials K p 2)

/--
Resolved: Yamaguchi, Ogawa and Takemura proved the Diaconis-Eriksson conjecture that the Markov
degree of the Birkhoff model is three (their Theorem 2.1, the case `r = n`): for `p ≥ 3` and any
field, the toric ideal of the permutation matrices is generated by binomials of degree two and
three, and every generating set contains a binomial of degree three, so the degree-`≤ 2` binomials
do not generate it. Source: AIM workshop Computational algebraic statistics, problem list,
https://aimath.org/WWN/compalgstat/compalgstat.pdf, Problem 1; resolution: T. Yamaguchi, M. Ogawa
and A. Takemura, Markov degree of the Birkhoff model, J. Algebraic Combin. 40 (2014), 293-311
(preprint METR 2013-05, University of Tokyo), Theorem 2.1 (with r = n): for n ≥ 3 the toric ideal of
the Birkhoff model is generated by binomials of degree two and three, and any set of generators
contains a binomial of degree three (proving the conjecture of Diaconis and Eriksson that the Markov
degree is three), https://doi.org/10.1007/s10801-013-0488-z.
-/
public theorem_wanted birkhoffMarkovDegreeThree_holds : birkhoffMarkovDegreeThree

end MathlibExt.Algebra.BirkhoffMarkovDegreeAIMComputationProblem16Wanted
